392611} :answer-field/id #uuid "540d3f99-dea4-52b1-a147-cd36aac0c02b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392609}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c05827a9-fdcf-58d8-bfea-b9a09196b4ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-48081" :question/problem "Select the function that is continuous at $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is shown below.  Indeed, the graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nAll the other given graphs contain breaks at $x=2$ and it would be impossible to draw them in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking the pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233061 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62ec00bb-a7dc-5ae8-ae64-a716f57687a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-102106" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9cf9a5c61cbf1796cf6682b129b4c05171c364c7b34e7cce176ba27e9c9cb24.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. - $x=-1$\nII. - $x=2$\nIII. - $x=4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=4.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=4,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and II only\".\n\n![](images/95/95967eb82512aae261936a6c09e2f474c72c9a130d3ecb527f62408822d88c96.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234403 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9857052-b60b-5b90-a22b-cd84e32dabbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-78133" :question/problem "![](images/43/43544a1ba403055094cae54fed9772df353d3697f50888728ebee61e77b729da.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. - $x=-7$\nII. - $x=-4$\nIII. - $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-7.$ Because there is a jump at $x=-7,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-7$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-4.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ Since there is a break at $x=-1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is: \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205e1e30-bd9c-50eb-9cb8-7a0cfa8d844e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236026} {:answer/id #uuid "55075645-dbcf-5f0e-b2df-212debdec0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236360} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9d5b00-dd0c-5d54-892b-57dbbaf60d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237010} {:answer/id #uuid "c1212b72-b517-5a23-a3ee-15d5d71a8557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237043} {:answer/id #uuid "fac8b27f-38bb-5d09-bf82-a4782974c9da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba9d5b00-dd0c-5d54-892b-57dbbaf60d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237010} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9b2dffa-7952-59b0-931a-25debad78721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237700}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3b92036-278a-5fb6-a1d2-39caf6b31601" :question/math-academy-id "q-102115" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1d91b2d791d69c512a6bd2e7d523563de076a7f3afda53513234e799152ef8d.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=1$\n- $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ Since there is a jump at $x=-1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ Since there is a break at $x=1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"III only\".\n\n \n\n![](images/57/57415045f7a1cc1c896756ff19cb17f90074151a25d12c56068fb6f86a064753.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68f016cd-f1df-5564-b277-b9ece07b1add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/42e1d99095fd174c1e9eb1835cf0a26f16d58ce9392a7654ad0fd0526fd35add.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333951} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9f44b0-368f-559a-b2a5-34548d645434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d124370f929cdd5f42cabe0f2588e668fb90a8e8abbd2140fe9db33d3e3b2fbf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334006} {:answer/id #uuid "e6fbc9e1-c840-5d83-b28b-d761b9044e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/17fc3