easy} :question/id #uuid "f6e088c5-8b59-5061-af03-e9b40ee3d2a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48088" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e111be8806fd5351b873219d9a790b71ca862a8fdef8b15df6477265cbb7ddea.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=-3$ and $x=0$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-3$ and $x=0.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068549 :question/id #uuid "01bca086-0ed4-5626-a357-e0724685f144" :question/math-academy-id "e-3569" :question/problem "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\n- $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph, we can see that the left and right-sided limits are different:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false.  The graph of the function breaks at $x=2.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048943 :topic/math-academy-id 625 :topic/title "Finding the Existence of a Limit Using One-Sided Limits"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051049 :topic/math-academy-id 1814 :topic/title "Infinite Limits from Graphs"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7e85ee-6515-5fb8-a5a2-074cbc9108df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327048} {:answer/id #uuid "29de8dce-e7c7-5fc2-94a7-5179e0c2f602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327294} {:answer/id #uuid "440c0fb1-d639-5470-acec-fe52787433f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327530} {:answer/id #uuid "937287c6-1b15-5c81-835f-d6dddc1cd449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328220} {:answer/id #uuid "fe29cc74-1bf5-5fbc-b9ad-88af91611a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d7e85ee-6515-5fb8-a5a2-074cbc9108df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327048} :answer-field/id #uuid "62261799-63f8-547f-a1a7-46cbd5868baa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329357}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "544abde9-c223-5348-b980-33df85581d2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48042" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8eb53506d9e01d09fe3e072a2c6ed1ade05e1cc185693bdd66b34bdf75797ad9.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ is finite\n- $f(x)$  has a discontinuity at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function contains a break exactly at $x=2$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a26128fd-a27e-5a35-8575-639ff9311589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067373} {:answer/id #uuid "b3835136-311e-52c3-8f9d-f243c85a4875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067374} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f192a9-8305-5e68-97be-10e4dac05d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067375} {:answer/id #uuid "330a48ae-091d-566c-827d-bd6e6174a48a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067376} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7c3791-ee87-5538-8024-8ad1d642d14a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067377}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1a24ceb9-b141-5695-8ef8-3816685f8054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067372}] :question/id #uuid "a302c934-1e7e-57a4-882d-8cd125952f61" :question/math-academy-id "q-48064" :question/problem "![](images/02/0218a1e13a0504b20e802100f9e15877beeb2b565a49fe7cd01cf2a0cf0197ee.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is defined at $x = 2$\n2. $lim_(x → 2)f(x)$ exists\n3. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234087 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2832c750-5223-5055-a543-69f3bfc7bf6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48063" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5ba5f7cc2ea5aaf405db1abff7e6f28ed4378ba6a5ec88d73c6861ede1d75de.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. - $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ exists\nIII. - $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of $f(x)$ breaks at $x=2$,  it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97842440-8562-5084-8515-ac9bed4f30cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239366} {:answer/id #uuid "adf67485-98f6-5532-b74e-ad997abb4115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239564} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f0ae0b-0e27-5e62-ba20-05fe58021513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239664} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1ddd11-773f-5bba-9c4f-50345066fd1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239859} {:answer/id #uuid "edb1bc67-aa4a-5b31-9afb-59791c7d655e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edb1bc67-aa4a-5b31-9afb-59791c7d655e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240149} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b0cf691-b10b-512c-8716-cad8675c07cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240407}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "340a8e11-ca0d-5e08-ab08-b9647c00e4dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-104049" :question/problem "![](images/51/5143df65d0f99c6511dc8ab13262831669c88535e19c986dacdb364e04847f58.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$\n- $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=2$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8bd6dd-1931-5df3-803b-dc0e5167142e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238463} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8ed7c5-b2af-580b-8113-c798acb35065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238721} {:answer/id #uuid "92b58f29-e9d2-52ea-80a1-a85417e0693d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239321} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b9cbc0-4187-50a5-81fd-c6539f6fe9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239932} {:answer/id #uuid "f9aeed86-3876-53b5-b2e9-7b048f60598b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9aeed86-3876-53b5-b2e9-7b048f60598b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240264} :answer-field/id #uuid "42c90896-bfe8-504a-9692-0915b93a4eb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240445}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc9393f2-809c-5769-b329-186102da51c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-104051" :question/problem "![](images/65/65b9b32e8fac6a71141ce820855058367e9235129de7334db35a200905474cc0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=5$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(5)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a jump exactly at $x=5$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013b8848-3885-5ddc-abba-c6d98f5249e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238091} {:answer/id #uuid "60b0fbff-7602-5867-b0a7-49ff7cf237ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238904} {:answer/id #uuid "ac75e25e-87f7-560b-989e-a0db688ca52b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239554} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fdba29-79ec-5716-8a2d-1c3ed48f88b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239755} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb22f50-3d68-5e9a-9196-af09faba8f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60b0fbff-7602-5867-b0a7-49ff7cf237ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238904} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9e6d376-017a-5f97-89b1-398a2e796dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240658}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3312f9fe-c2a1-5d75-b752-6c981a0a3f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104052" :question/problem "![](images/80/8086893e4587e68f8257b75f2070cf622fa141199d6bd8960f7c4fd8e7bcb349.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=0$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & =3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is not even defined at $x=0.$ The graph contains a break at $x=0.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240800 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d0261a4-d877-5906-ad0b-830747a5cc4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239135} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad4d9e3-4681-5c2a-91a3-0ffe435a5b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239261} {:answer/id #uuid "b164497e-772a-5260-941e-feac495b0767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239604} {:answer/id #uuid "bb707977-2299-58a7-be64-009156d71aed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239688} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c3ed1b-b297-5c48-93a5-4e4568f8c09c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb707977-2299-58a7-be64-009156d71aed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239688} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0db112b-2842-5c5e-b64c-f115b941ce9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240639}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad98348d-cbe6-535a-9828-f9008c2ec2e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104124" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bdcfa35fb04028cbff32992b41214c878443d3815b676a0cf046f5a99bd771bd.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=-2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=f(-2)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x) & =5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=5=f(-2).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19eb6e8d-8d23-5950-9d1f-719568b31714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327158} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc07d22-ae7a-5837-bd01-69ca3c1713a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328314} {:answer/id #uuid "cca94791-290b-5089-b6f7-05b72b8ffcf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328728} {:answer/id #uuid "d5090347-0ef4-5430-bfdb-5390e21fb3ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328798} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f1caf8-4682-580c-b7d8-ee546c5a8d5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5090347-0ef4-5430-bfdb-5390e21fb3ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328798} :answer-field/id #uuid "78f6c570-d96e-503c-820b-a483ee09771d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329384}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18223e97-17ad-509c-a688-bf8d869b747a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48040" :question/problem "![](images/59/59c02273515e87fcebb8eb1785dc2cf0c6490feda4fc9644df43b8849921eec5.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$ and we have $f(-1)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, both left-hand side and right-hand side limits exist but they are not the same. Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function contains a break exactly at $x=-1$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee867b9-645a-512d-9f99-f3aa8226ad71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :a