possible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f05bd8f-eb2f-57d7-ae18-c11817bd78b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327620} {:answer/id #uuid "83335e31-f97c-5df4-863a-ef88150fb6f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328065} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9a91aa-3ed8-5151-bcb5-e59ccd13d6bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328272} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9e8871-1f30-5744-8374-042f56698b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328750} {:answer/id #uuid "d6472410-aa22-5e41-a0c0-09e47c3fb93c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83335e31-f97c-5df4-863a-ef88150fb6f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328065} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e84077-b88d-5119-bcf7-6c45cbfd0278" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329249}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e01edc7-98f4-52fe-bf43-cb1e05ae0d4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48065" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa15192923f9fd7370818fcb6b05b24b3bb6901dfbf90dd53539d71fd0f8b0bf.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=f(2)$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$ and $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b74aed1-5750-566e-973a-b9fe0109c876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327995} {:answer/id #uuid "98ce5d05-688f-5cf5-9b21-352ade35d261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328254} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf58768-099b-5e1e-ba54-551e1eec0a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328289} {:answer/id #uuid "b440b75f-1cc0-592d-87f4-cd361fc03170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328501} {:answer/id #uuid "ef006457-c201-515f-919a-a5f8772d65f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b74aed1-5750-566e-973a-b9fe0109c876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327995} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fafe6c7-ed63-54d7-8bcd-351dbdd97823" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329309}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7aef8bfd-11ab-51f5-ad27-2faedcadc29e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48067" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c132c73b7ca4759882959f9df42110c36e31f3c8af27afa02c0caa20f5f94d52.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=f(1)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(1)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1=f(1).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18eaadf1-683f-5951-8da7-2c8b04aa572f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327151} {:answer/id #uuid "97125a5f-4f67-5544-94dd-ed787cd4805a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328242} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7f71f4-41c8-5d9e-b405-045f8fa54bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328409} {:answer/id #uuid "f92419cf-94b1-5b46-bc59-f5aee0d957ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329120} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8882da-83f4-5fd5-a9fa-309e57cccf12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa7f71f4-41c8-5d9e-b405-045f8fa54bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328409} :answer-field/id #uuid "d63a5833-0377-5a91-a4df-afbabcc49049" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329564}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f506060c-db8a-5e26-aad7-74b6b6b44f61" :question/math-academy-id "q-48068" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a3874b7907227653d93451d9ce0960fdadc8cb3e10396e0f27f7ebb363bebe2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ is **not** finite\n- $f(x)$  is  continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=-1$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068779 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068619 :question/id #uuid "d86c6bd2-e528-55ed-bbfc-9f5ecbec024e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5924" :question/problem "Graph the function $y = -\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y = -\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/9d/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png)\n\n- Translate it by one unit to the right to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x-1}.$ \n\n![](images/8c/8ce8c53f202f04b3b34a247b797f65f7616e8772afa1751de126ddd401417bd7.png)\n\nNotice that the vertical asymptote is now $x=1.$\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{2}{x-1}.$\n\n![](images/c9/c9b708a20d9c6c12f0016279e8d7a3ea55597e7645f0a9518f1c32e8a8406e47.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit down to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1.$ \n\n![](images/e1/e100b552adb71cae1295492a58b0e60525813adb87d9815ba64a151a856f0c46.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote is now $y=-1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4640f978-9caa-5960-bec4-f153a1b4cc5d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisite